 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The time complexity for computing the convoluted components is $O(I \cdot J \cdot L_N + J \cdot K \cdot L_N + K \cdot I \cdot L_N)$, assuming the 2D Gaussian convolution on matrix components is separated into 1D Gaussian convolutions. The time complexity for each query sample is $O(R)$ [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Space Complexity:** The space complexity is maintained at the level of the decomposed tensor, avoiding the $O(I \cdot J \cdot K)$ space required to store the full 3D volume during convolution [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Comparison with Naive Approach:** Naive 3D convolution has a time complexity of $O(I \cdot J \cdot K \cdot L_N^3)$ and space complexity of $O(I \cdot J \cdot K)$ [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Comparison with Traditional Separated Kernel:** Traditional separated kernel convolution has a time complexity of $O(I \cdot J \cdot K \cdot L_N)$ and space complexity of $O(I \cdot J \cdot K)$ [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Drastically reduces computation required for filtering 3D radiance fields while maintaining the space compression advantage of decomposed low-rank tensors [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires the input volume to be in a decomposed low-rank tensor format. The method relies on the specific identity (Theorem 4) which may not apply to all tensor decomposition schemes.
+- **Time Complexity:** Computing convoluted components is $O(I \cdot J \cdot L_N + J \cdot K \cdot L_N + K \cdot I \cdot L_N)$, assuming 2D Gaussian convolution on matrix components is separated into 1D Gaussian convolutions [S1][S2, unofficial]. Each query sample is $O(R)$ [S1][S2, unofficial].
+- **Space Complexity:** $O(I \cdot J + J \cdot K + K \cdot I + I \cdot J \cdot K \cdot R)$ for storing the decomposed components and their convolved versions, significantly less than the $O(I \cdot J \cdot K)$ required for full volume reconstruction [S1][S2, unofficial].
+- **Performance:** Drastically reduces computation required for filtering 3D radiance fields compared to naive or traditional methods [S1][S2, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Preserves space compression of decomposed low-rank tensors; computationally efficient for large volumes; enables coarse-to-fine training schedules [S1][S2, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Requires the input to be in decomposed low-rank tensor format; relies on the validity of the component-wise convolution identity (Theorem 4) [S1][S2, unofficial].
+- **When to use:** When applying Gaussian smoothing to decomposed low-rank tensor volumes (e.g., TensoRF) for spectrum limiting in radiance field optimization [S1][S2, unofficial].
+- **When not to use:** When the data is not in decomposed low-rank tensor format, or when full 3D convolution is explicitly required for non-separable operations [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method was proposed in the context of improving robustness for joint optimization of camera poses and decomposed low-rank tensorial radiance fields. It addresses the issue that naively convolving a 3D Gaussian kernel with a 3D volume destroys the space compression advantage of decomposed low-rank tensors. The technique leverages an identity (Theorem 4) to distribute the 3D Gaussian convolution across individual components of the decomposed tensor. This allows for efficient spectrum limiting of the radiance field with a coarse-to-fine training schedule [S1, unofficial].
+The method was proposed in the context of improving robustness for joint optimization of camera pose and decomposed low-rank tensorial radiance fields, specifically addressing issues with inaccurate poses and suboptimal reconstruction quality [S1][S2, unofficial]. It was introduced in the paper "Improving Robustness for Joint Optimization of Camera Pose and Decomposed Low-Rank Tensorial Radiance Fields" published at AAAI-24 [S1]. The method leverages the separability of the decomposed low-rank tensor structure to perform efficient 3D Gaussian convolution, avoiding the need to reconstruct the full 3D volume [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
